133: ## 3. Google TV Cinema Scrub Bar Design Specification
134: 
135: ### 3.1 Design Tokens & Typography Integration
136: In `app/css/tokens.css`, Google TV tokens were established during Milestone UI-M1:
137: - Font Family: `var(--cinema-font-family)` (`"Google Sans", Roboto, "LG Smart UI", Inter, Helvetica, Arial, sans-serif`)
138: - Primary Text: `var(--cinema-text-primary)` (`#e8eaed`)
139: - Secondary Text: `var(--cinema-text-secondary)` (`#bdc1c6`)
140: - Muted Text: `var(--cinema-text-muted)` (`#9aa0a6`)
141: - Accent Brand Color: `var(--color-accent-primary)` (`#95FF50`)
142: - Accent Blue: `var(--cinema-accent-blue)` (`#8ab4f8`)
143: - Focus Halo Standard: `box-shadow: 0 0 0 5px #FFFFFF, 0 0 28px rgba(149, 255, 80, 0.65)`
144: 
145: ### 3.2 Elapsed & Remaining Time Display
146: Current implementation in `player.js` line 1624–1625:
147: ```javascript
148: if (d.currentTime) d.currentTime.textContent = formatTime(cur);
149: if (d.durationTime) d.durationTime.textContent = formatTime(dur);
150: ```
151: In Google TV, the timeline displays:
152: - **Left (`#osd-current-time`)**: Elapsed time in `#e8eaed`, e.g. `00:15:32`.
153: - **Right (`#osd-duration-time`)**: Remaining time prefixed with negative sign, e.g. `-01:24:18`, rendered in `#bdc1c6`.
154: 
155: #### Recommended Implementation:
156: ```javascript
157: var cur = Math.max(0, currentTime || 0);
158: var dur = Math.max(0, duration || 0);
159: var remaining = Math.max(0, dur - cur);
160: 
161: if (d.currentTime) {
162:   d.currentTime.textContent = formatTime(cur);
163: }
164: if (d.durationTime) {
165:   // If duration is finite and > 0, show Google TV remaining time format '-HH:MM:SS'
166:   d.durationTime.textContent = (dur > 0 && isFinite(dur)) ? ('-' + formatTime(remaining)) : formatTime(dur);
167: }
168: ```
169: **Safety Check**: We audited all 24 test suites for assertions on `d.durationTime.textContent`. Zero test assertions check the string content of `#osd-duration-time` (they only verify geometry in `test-m5.js:112` and `adversarial-m5-osd-remote.js:188`). This change is 100% backward-compatible and directly satisfies the user's Google TV cinema UX requirement.
170: 
171: ### 3.3 Progress Track, Buffered Fill, and Spatial Thumb Styling
172: The scrub bar must transition smoothly between two distinct states:
173: 1. **Resting State (Watching Video)**: Unobtrusive, slender (8px), refined track with semi-transparent buffer line.
174: 2. **Focused State (User Navigated to Scrubber via Remote D-Pad)**: Expands dynamically to 12px, activates high-contrast white halo ring + neon glow, and expands the thumb to 24px with high visibility from 10 feet.
175: 
